6235729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235291 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186235735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235291 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186235778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235291 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186235780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235291 #edb/ref 1085 "![](images/e4/e489437aa3b22a90d71dea3d30ff7a53bb800580775666f97dd4a6cf2db76e6d.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235291 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "2eaab060-c168-5f4b-9fce-1c85507d3036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235291 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186235780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235335 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9430395f-82c4-5ad6-bbe0-d4084dabc6c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235335 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186234960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235374 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131476" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235374 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235374 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f97091e5-4c8a-5c29-84a6-a20e717682cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235374 #edb/ref 1074 "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235375 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235375 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235375 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235375 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235375 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a23363d4-1539-5b65-9754-68c957b99234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235375 #edb/ref 1074 "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235376 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bd1cc7e-1467-5fa9-9364-b08bd7aa89f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235376 #edb/ref 1074 "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $-1.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $-1.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-1,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=-1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235377 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47e117ef-6738-5bd8-aeaa-a7a0343ceecf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235377 #edb/ref 1074 "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=3.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $3$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=3.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function decrease without bound to $-\\infty .$   Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235378 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=\\frac{4}{3},$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6ba6327-0941-5856-a970-a0df483bddf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235378 #edb/ref 1074 "From the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=4.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}Cf(x) & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C\\cdot 4 & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235379 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235379 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235379 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235379 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(x)+8g(x)-2{x}^{3}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(x)+8g(x)-2{x}^{3})=$ \n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235379 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1547780d-30a4-520c-aff8-9cc9f3435542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235379 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(x)+8g(x)-2{x}^{3})=7{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)+8{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)-2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe above equation assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nIn part 5, we found that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=4,$ and from part 3, we found ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=-1.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(7f(x)+8g(x)-2{x}^{3}) & =7{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)+8{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)-2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+8\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =28-8-16 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235380 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235380 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131482" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235380 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235380 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the points of discontinuity of $f(x)?$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235380 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c961b194-602b-5753-8bf9-ef5e3612c107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235380 #edb/ref 1074 "The point of discontinuity of $f(x)$ is $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in a neighborhood of this point without taking our pencil off of the page." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235381 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235381 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186235281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235381 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235381 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235381 #edb/ref 1071 "**In ascending order**, what are the points of discontinuity of $g(x)?$\n\n$g(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235381 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30c51f0c-7f0a-55e0-9cee-18ad253ce706" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235381 #edb/ref 1074 "The points of discontinuity of $g(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these points without taking our pencil off of the page." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235618 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186235622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235618 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b6aba97-c86a-561f-a073-e2fcde9dc0b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235618 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186235377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235622 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186235729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235622 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2dc4f58b-f875-571d-bf59-0b93403483b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235622 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186235378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235639 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186235735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235639 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40bcbf58-da94-56b1-a4f7-39bf6ff224e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235639 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186235380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235654 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186235778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235654 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "527e09d0-fc60-5695-afb3-ad665a241a9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235654 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186235375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235729 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186235