ing our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=- \\frac{1}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=- \\frac{1}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{1}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](images/4d/4db3d16c1d05cf201e94dcbb7a8e53f82e412aa9d70a72c9766022b5d2d79466.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513496 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513497 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186513500 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/14/1449fe787d2263ffd6a0d11450b34425aa4f8b0e4bd4cec0ad3a516b3b85df0c.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Use the unit-spaced tick marks to locate the listed points. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=- \\frac{9}{2}$\n- **II.** $x=-3$\n- **III.** $x=- \\frac{3}{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "304021dc-0557-5932-b360-6e01c0194b41" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186513495 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=- \\frac{9}{2}.$ Because there is a jump at $x=- \\frac{9}{2},$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=- \\frac{9}{2}$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=- \\frac{3}{2}.$ Since there is a break at $x=- \\frac{3}{2},$ it is impossible t